    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "“Shippo-yaki” is the Japanese term for metal enamelware, a technique derived from Chinese cloisonné that involves fusing glassy enamel onto a metal base. Founded in Nagoya in 1880 (Meiji 13), Ando Cloisonné was designated an official supplier to the Imperial Household Agency in 1900 (Meiji 33), cementing its status as one of Japan’s most renowned cloisonné masters.",
      "url": null,
      "full_url": null,
      "alt": null,
      "columns": 2,
      "items": []
    },
